# ---------------- CommandCard.events (Task 29 Part E) ----------------
# One shared RTS command card: it reads what is selected (Task 29 Part D) and orders the whole
# selection, instead of every unit sheet carrying its own copy of the buttons.
#
# SETUP (editor) — the card lives in the gameplay scene. `Content/scenes/RTS_Test_0.scene`
# already has this whole setup (canvas, seven buttons, the manager, the sheet, a Barracks and two
# Marines on placeholder geometry), so it is the working example to copy from:
#   · one actor carries this sheet: an EventSheet whose Event File is `CommandCard`. It can sit on
#     the same actor as the scene's EventsManager; button presses are broadcast by name to every
#     sheet, so which actor holds it does not matter.
#   · a UICanvas with six Button UIControls named `StopButton`, `HoldButton`, `TrainMarineButton`,
#     `CancelMarineButton`, `LiftButton`, `LandButton` (the research button is optional).
#   · the card must be in the SAME scene as the units: exactly one EventsManager runs per scene
#     (a second one is disabled with a warning), and its button poll only sees its own scene — so
#     do not put the card in an additive HUD scene with its own manager. Attach the sheet to the
#     manager actor in the gameplay scene instead.
#   · per-actor labels stay on the unit/building sheets: a card sheet cannot read another actor's
#     `my train time` or `my "stim" cooldown`, so `Marine.events` keeps `bind StimLabel ...` and
#     `Barracks.events` keeps `bind ResearchLabel ...`.
#   · a building dropped into a scene by hand needs `set my Building to 1` in `when me appears`
#     before `selection is all buildings` can be true of it — `Barracks.events` already does this.
#
# WHAT THIS SHEET DOES NOT DO YET (recorded in tasks_RTS.md Part E notes):
#   · Move / Attack / Attack-Move buttons need a targeting cursor (press the button, then click),
#     which no word offers yet — the right click already issues those orders (Task 29 Part C).
#   · An ability button (Stim) cannot reuse the unit's own `when Q is pressed` block: there is no
#     word that runs another sheet's block for every selected unit. Stim stays on `Marine.events`.
#   · The card cannot grey a button *because of one specific reason* — a body has no way to say
#     "if unaffordable print this, if the queue is full print that". Train greys out for both
#     reasons, and `train` already warns which one it was when the queue is full.

# Nothing selected, nothing on screen. A hidden button also stops polling, so it cannot be clicked
# while it belongs to the other layout.
when scene starts
  hide StopButton
  hide HoldButton
  hide TrainMarineButton
  hide CancelMarineButton
  hide LiftButton
  hide LandButton
  hide ResearchButton

# The layout is chosen from the selection, not from C#: everything selected is a unit -> the unit
# buttons, everything is a building -> the building buttons, nothing selected -> neither.
when selection changes
  hide StopButton
  hide HoldButton
  hide TrainMarineButton
  hide CancelMarineButton
  hide LiftButton
  hide LandButton
  hide ResearchButton
  if selection is all units
  show StopButton
  show HoldButton
  if selection is all buildings
  show TrainMarineButton
  show CancelMarineButton
  show LiftButton
  show LandButton
  show ResearchButton

# ---- unit layout ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Move, attack and attack-move are right-click orders (Task 29 Part C), so the card only carries
# the orders that have no click of their own.
when StopButton is pressed
  selection is all units
  selected units stop

when HoldButton is pressed
  selection is all units
  selected units hold position

# ---- building layout -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# One press trains one Marine at every selected Barracks, the StarCraft rule. The three stacked
# gates are the same ones `Barracks.events` uses on its W key, so a card press and a hotkey can
# never disagree with what the engine will accept.
when TrainMarineButton is pressed
  selection is all buildings
  can afford Marine
  queue has room
  print "Card: train Marine"
  selected buildings train Marine

when CancelMarineButton is pressed
  selection is all buildings
  selected buildings cancel training Marine

when LiftButton is pressed
  selection is all buildings
  print "Card: lift off"
  selected buildings lift off

when LandButton is pressed
  selection is all buildings
  print "Card: land"
  selected buildings land

# The price comes from Upgrades.events, so it is checked by the manager rather than by
# `can afford` (which reads live globals only) — an unaffordable press is refused with a warning.
when ResearchButton is pressed
  selection is all buildings
  selected buildings research "Stim Pack"

# ---- affordability / cap feedback ----------------------------------------------------------
# Train is the one button whose state is data. A greyed button is `Control.Enabled = false`, which
# is also what stops the mouse from pressing it; the blocks are ordered so the last word wins —
# disabled when the layout is wrong, when the unit is unaffordable, or when the queue is full, and
# enabled only when all three are true.
when every 0.5 seconds
  unless selection is all buildings
  set button TrainMarineButton disabled

when every 0.5 seconds
  selection is all buildings
  unless can afford Marine
  set button TrainMarineButton disabled

when every 0.5 seconds
  selection is all buildings
  unless queue has room
  set button TrainMarineButton disabled

when every 0.5 seconds
  selection is all buildings
  can afford Marine
  queue has room
  set button TrainMarineButton enabled
